          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Let ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "f(x)=\\frac{ax+b}{cx+d}",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " and ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "g(x)=\\frac{bx+a}{dx+c}",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ".",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p91-q53-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that the vertical asymptote of $g$ is the reciprocal of the vertical asymptote of $f$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For $c,d\\ne0$ and $ad\\ne bc$, the vertical asymptotes are $x=-\\frac{d}{c}$ for $f$ and $x=-\\frac{c}{d}$ for $g$. Their $x$-values are reciprocals.\n\nSet each denominator equal to zero; $ad\\ne bc$ prevents cancellation.",
          "worked": "The stated claim requires $c,d\\ne0$ and $ad\\ne bc$. Set each denominator equal to zero:\n$$\\begin{align*}cx+d&=0\\\\x&=-\\frac{d}{c}\\\\dx+c&=0\\\\x&=-\\frac{c}{d}\\end{align*}$$\nSince $ad\\ne bc$, neither denominator zero cancels with a numerator zero. Thus these are genuine vertical asymptotes. Their positions satisfy\n$$\\left(-\\frac{d}{c}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{c}{d}\\right)=1,$$\nso the vertical-asymptote positions are reciprocals.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 54
      },
      {
        "id": "p91-q53-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that the $y$-intercept of $g$ is the horizontal asymptote of $f$, and vice versa.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For $c,d\\ne0$, $g$ has $y$-intercept $(0,\\frac{a}{c})$ and $f$ has horizontal asymptote $y=\\frac{a}{c}$. Likewise, $f$ has $y$-intercept $(0,\\frac{b}{d})$ and $g$ has horizontal asymptote $y=\\frac{b}{d}$.\n\nThe intercept ordinates equal the opposite asymptote levels.",
          "worked": "Assume $c,d\\ne0$. Substitute $x=0$ for the intercepts and use the leading-coefficient ratios for the horizontal limits:\n$$\\begin{align*}g(0)&=\\frac{a}{c}\\\\\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}f(x)&=\\frac{a}{c}\\\\f(0)&=\\frac{b}{d}\\\\\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}g(x)&=\\frac{b}{d}\\end{align*}$$\nThus $g$ has $y$-intercept $(0,\\frac{a}{c})$, while $f$ has horizontal asymptote $y=\\frac{a}{c}$. Similarly, $f$ has $y$-intercept $(0,\\frac{b}{d})$, while $g$ has horizontal asymptote $y=\\frac{b}{d}$. The equality is between each intercept's $y$-coordinate and the other function's horizontal-asymptote level.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 56
      },
      {
        "id": "p91-q53-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Show that $g(x)=f\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$g(x)=f\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)$ wherever $x\\ne0$ and $dx+c\\ne0$.\n\nSubstitute $\\frac{1}{x}$ into $f$ and multiply the numerator and denominator by $x$.",
          "worked": "For $x\\ne0$ and $dx+c\\ne0$, substitute $\\frac{1}{x}$ into $f$ and multiply the numerator and denominator by $x$:\n$$\\begin{align*}f\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)&=\\frac{\\frac{a}{x}+b}{\\frac{c}{x}+d}\\\\f\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)&=\\frac{a+bx}{c+dx}\\\\f\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)&=g(x)\\end{align*}$$\nThis establishes the identity on the common domain. When $c\\ne0$, $g(0)$ exists, but $f(\\frac{1}{x})$ is undefined at $x=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      },
      {
        "id": "p91-q53-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Use this relationship to explain the results shown in previous parts.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For $c,d\\ne0$ and $ad\\ne bc$, reciprocal input sends the nonzero vertical-asymptote position $-\\frac{d}{c}$ to $-\\frac{c}{d}$. It also exchanges behaviour near zero with behaviour at infinity, giving $g(0)=\\frac{a}{c}$ from $f$'s horizontal level and $g$'s horizontal level $\\frac{b}{d}$ from $f(0)$.\n\nUse limits and continuity at zero; the identity itself requires $x\\ne0$ and $dx+c\\ne0$.",
          "worked": "Assume $c,d\\ne0$ and $ad\\ne bc$. On the common domain $x\\ne0$ and $dx+c\\ne0$, write $u=\\frac{1}{x}$, so $g(x)=f(u)$.\n\nThe nonzero vertical-asymptote position $u=-\\frac{d}{c}$ corresponds to\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{x}&=-\\frac{d}{c}\\\\x&=-\\frac{c}{d}.\\end{align*}$$\nThis explains the reciprocal positions in part (a).\n\nAs $x\\to0^\\pm$, $u\\to\\pm\\infty$, so $g(x)\\to \\frac{a}{c}$. Since $c\\ne0$, $g$ is continuous at zero, giving $g(0)=\\frac{a}{c}$. Thus the horizontal-asymptote level of $f$ becomes the intercept ordinate of $g$.\n\nAs $x\\to\\pm\\infty$, $u\\to0^\\pm$. Since $d\\ne0$, $f$ is continuous at zero, so\n$$\\begin{align*}\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}g(x)&=f(0)\\\\\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}g(x)&=\\frac{b}{d}.\\end{align*}$$\nThus the intercept ordinate of $f$ becomes the horizontal-asymptote level of $g$, completing the explanation of part (b).",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      }
    ]
  },
